Dear All Things Additive Editorial Team,

Solidon3D 0.2.2 connects three desktop polymer-printing tasks that are often
handled separately: adapting imported geometry, assigning the physical
material configuration and handing structured data to an external slicer.

The workflow begins with a recognised feature in an imported STL or 3MF. A
mounting hole can be resized while its original location and axis are
preserved, and the edit remains reversible. Standards-based inserts, screw
seats or bearing seats can then use clearance values measured from a printed
calibration artefact.

Each real filament spool carries colour, temperatures, cooling, retraction and
material properties. The application writes those values into the correct 3MF
material slot and maps them into the slicer handoff, avoiding a mismatch
between a selected colour and another spool's process settings.

The release also adds more capable constrained sketching, matching printable
fasteners and reusable parametric components built from operation history.
Solidon is local desktop preparation software rather than a slicer or
enterprise data platform. It runs on Windows, macOS and Linux without an
account, telemetry or cloud project store.

Overview: https://solidon3d.de/en/

